<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mindanao</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mindanao is the second-largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, in the south of the archipelago. Its western and southern parts were home to Muslim peoples and the Sultanate of Maguindanao, which, with the nearby Sulu Sultanate, resisted Spanish conquest for more than three centuries. Muslim regions of the island now form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangsamoro">Bangsamoro</a> Autonomous Region, created in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mindanao.jpg" alt="Mindanao" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ESA Copernicus Sentinel-2, processed by EOX · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mindanao_Sentinel-2_MSI_2019_cloudless_composite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mindanao covers 97,530 square kilometres and is mountainous; Mount Apo is its highest peak and the highest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Four seas surround it: the Sulu Sea to the west, the Philippine Sea to the east, the Celebes Sea to the south and the Mindanao Sea to the north. The upland plateaus of Bukidnon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanao">Lanao</a> del Sur hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lanao">Lake Lanao</a>, the largest lake on the island, and two large lowlands lie in the valleys of the Agusan River and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> de Mindanao. The name comes from the Maguindanao language: min, a past-tense prefix, and danao, 'lake'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds show human activity about ten thousand years ago, and Austronesian peoples spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> around 1500 BCE. Caves in the Zamboanga Peninsula held burial jars, celadons of the Yuan and Ming periods and gold ornaments, which point to long trade between the Subanon and the Chinese. From about 900 CE the people of Mindanao were strongly influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist belief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and Malaysia, and the island has yielded a golden kinnara and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> pendant.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from the 14th century through merchants from the western Malay Archipelago, and the first mosque in the country was built in the mid-14th century at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simunul">Simunul</a>. About the 16th century the sultanates of Sulu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanao">Lanao</a> and Maguindanao grew out of earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> rajahnates. In 1521 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-pigafetta">Antonio Pigafetta</a>, who sailed with Magellan, wrote of reaching the island. In 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sold the archipelago to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for 20 million dollars. Settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a> were encouraged to move to Mindanao in the early 1900s, and settlement displaced the Blaan and Tboli. Japanese forces invaded in April 1942 and burned many towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mindanao is divided into six administrative regions, and its largest cities include Davao City, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga City</a>, Cagayan de Oro and General Santos. Dozens of languages are spoken, of which Cebuano has the most speakers. Most residents are Christian, and Muslims form the largest minority. The island is a major supplier of pineapples and bananas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mindanao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sulu Sultanate grew out of the trading chiefdoms of the Sulu archipelago, the chain of islands between <b>Mindanao</b> and northern Borneo inhabited by the Tausug and Sama peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It does not take into account the many other native house forms, such as those of the rulers of <b>Mindanao</b>, whose architecture shows strong Islamic influence.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bahay-kubo">Bahay Kubo</a></li><li><blockquote>Buayan was the upstream, sa raya, state of the Pulangi river basin in central <b>Mindanao</b>, while Maguindanao held the coast, sa ilud.</blockquote><a href="/state/buayan-sultanate">Buayan Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Muslim <b>Mindanao</b> and Sulu resisted conquest.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a settlement of the Bohol coast, probably on Panglao Island, ruled by datus and trading with other Visayan and <b>Mindanao</b> ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedatuan-of-dapitan">Kedatuan of Dapitan</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Kudarat, reigning from about 1619 to 1671, united much of <b>Mindanao</b>, fought Governor Sebastian Hurtado de Corcuera in 1637 and made peace with Spain on his own terms.</blockquote><a href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
